# XCU xargs
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Output is produced with echo and printf, which the test passes as the
# utility. -I and -L are XSI. -p reads /dev/tty and is not testable here.
# Command line lengths are those defined on the page: the bytes of the
# utility name and each argument, each with a terminating null byte.

assertion  XCU.xargs.001
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPERANDS
class      required
says       Without a utility operand xargs runs echo with the arguments read
           from standard input, and initial arguments of the utility operand
           come before them.
test       t/001-default-echo.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.002
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Without -0 arguments are separated by blanks and newlines (runs of
           separators count once), single quotes and double quotes keep blanks
           and the other quote character literal, and a backslash makes the
           next character literal.
test       t/002-delimiters-and-quotes.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.003
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       -n NUMBER runs the utility with at most NUMBER arguments each time
           and a final run with the remainder.
test       t/003-max-args.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.004
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       Without arguments on standard input the utility runs exactly once;
           with -r it does not run at all.
test       t/004-no-input.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.005
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       -E STRING makes an argument that is only STRING (after quote
           processing) the end of input and the arguments before it are
           used; -E with an empty string switches this off so an underscore is
           an ordinary argument.
test       t/005-eof-string.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.006
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       With -0 arguments end at null bytes only, so blanks, newlines and
           quote characters are part of the argument, and adjacent null bytes
           give an empty argument between them.
test       t/006-null-separated.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.007
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#STDERR
class      required
says       -t writes each command line to standard error, as the utility and
           its arguments, before running it, and the command still runs.
test       t/007-trace.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.008
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       -s SIZE keeps each command line, counted as utility name plus
           arguments with a null byte each, below SIZE bytes, so that fewer
           arguments are used per run when more would not fit.
test       t/008-size.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.009
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
class      required
says       With -x xargs terminates with a status between 1 and 125 when a
           command line with the requested number of arguments does not fit
           in the size; without -x it uses fewer arguments and succeeds.
test       t/009-exact-size.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.010
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       -I STR runs the utility once for each input line, replaces STR in
           the arguments by the whole line with leading blanks removed (a line
           may hold blanks), and replaces it in more than one argument.
test       t/010-replace.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.011
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
class      conditional
when       XSI
says       -L NUMBER runs the utility for each NUMBER input lines, ignoring
           empty lines, with a short last run, and a line ending in a blank
           continues onto the next line.
test       t/011-lines.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.012
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
says       xargs exits with status 127 when the utility cannot be found and
           126 when it is found but cannot be invoked.
test       t/012-not-found-not-executable.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.013
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
class      required
says       When an invocation of the utility exits with a non-zero status other
           than 255, xargs goes on with the remaining input and exits with a
           status between 1 and 125.
test       t/013-failing-utility.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.014
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       When an invocation exits with status 255, xargs writes a diagnostic
           message, processes no more input and exits with a status between 1
           and 125.
test       t/014-status-255.sh

assertion  XCU.xargs.015
anchor     utilities/xargs.html#OPTIONS
class      untestable
says       -p writes the command line and a prompt to standard error and runs
           the command only on an affirmative answer read from /dev/tty.
reason     The answer is read from the controlling terminal, which a script
           run by the harness has no way to supply.
